#doc (Manual) "Resolution of singularities in Mathlib's language" =>
%%%
tag := "ch-standard"
file := "standard-theorems"
%%%

The two theorems of this section state resolution of singularities in its most familiar forms,
using only Mathlib's definitions and three short ones given in full here. A reader who knows Mathlib
can check that they say what they claim without learning any of the library's own definitions.

- **Every variety in characteristic zero is resolved by a single blow-up**
  ({bpref "thm:standard_algebraic"}[]). An integral scheme, separated and of finite type over a
  field of characteristic zero, has a blow-up along an ideal sheaf supported exactly on its
  singular locus. The result is smooth over the field, and the map is proper and an isomorphism
  away from the singular locus. The blow-up is given by its universal property
  ({bpref "def:blowup_along"}[]), in terms of effective Cartier ideal sheaves
  ({bpref "def:effective_cartier"}[]), the two notions Mathlib lacks.
- **Every real-analytic function is locally a monomial after a proper change of variables**
  ({bpref "thm:standard_analytic"}[]). The Jacobian determinant of the change of variables is a
  monomial in the same coordinates, both up to a unit ({bpref "def:monomial_at"}[]). This is
  the form resolution takes in analysis, as in {citeAs Ati70}[Atiyah's division of distributions].

Both follow from the main theorems of the next two sections. Those theorems are stronger and
closer to their sources, but are stated in the library's own vocabulary.

# Resolution of a variety
%%%
tag := "sec-standard-algebraic"
%%%

::::definition "def:effective_cartier" (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsEffectiveCartier")
%%%
paperIdentity := some { label := "[Sta, Tag 01WR]", href := "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WR" }
%%%
An ideal sheaf $`J` on a scheme $`X` is *effective Cartier*, `J.IsEffectiveCartier`, the ideal
sheaf of an effective Cartier divisor, if near every point it is generated by a single
non-zero-divisor: every point has an affine open neighbourhood $`U` with $`J(U) = (g)` for a
non-zero-divisor $`g` of $`\Gamma(X, U)`. Equivalently, $`J` is an invertible sheaf (locally free
of rank one), which is the Stacks project's definition of an effective Cartier divisor
{cite Sta}[Tag 01WR]; {cite Sta}[Tag 01WS]. It is the library's invertible ideal sheaf
({bpref "def:invertible"}[]), restated from Mathlib's notions alone
(`isEffectiveCartier_iff_isInvertible`).
::::

::::definition "def:blowup_along" (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.IsBlowUpAlong")
%%%
paperIdentity := some { label := "[Sta, Tag 0806]", href := "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0806" }
%%%
A morphism $`\pi \colon B \to X` is a *blow-up of $`X` along an ideal sheaf $`I`*,
`π.IsBlowUpAlong I`, if the inverse image of the centre is an effective Cartier divisor (the
inverse image of $`I` on $`B` is effective Cartier, {bpref "def:effective_cartier"}[]) and every
$`f \colon Y \to X` along which the inverse image of the centre is an effective Cartier divisor
factors as $`f = \pi \circ g` for a unique $`g \colon Y \to B` {cite Sta}[Tag 0806]. The universal
property is the definition, so a statement asking for a blow-up needs no construction of it, and a
blow-up is unique up to unique isomorphism over $`X`. It is the library's predicate `IsBlowUp` of
the universal property ({ref "sec-universal"}[The universal property]), restated from Mathlib's
notions alone (`isBlowUpAlong_iff_isBlowUp`); the blow-up of the library
({bpref "def:blowup_construction"}[]) is a blow-up in this sense.
::::

::::theorem "thm:standard_algebraic" (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isBlowUpAlong_smooth")
%%%
paperIdentity := some { label := "[Hir64, Main Theorem I, p. 132]", href := "https://doi.org/10.2307/1970486" }
%%%
Let $`k` be a field of characteristic zero and $`X` an integral scheme, separated and of finite
type over $`k`. Then there is a blow-up $`\pi \colon X' \to X` along an ideal sheaf $`I` whose
support is exactly the singular locus of $`X`, the complement of the smooth locus $`X^{ns}`, with
$`X'` smooth over $`k`. In particular $`\pi` is proper and restricts to an isomorphism
$`\pi^{-1}(X^{ns}) \to X^{ns}`.

This is Hironaka's Main Theorem I ({bpref "thm:mt1"}[]): $`X` is resolved by the single blow-up
with centre its singular locus. Here a blow-up is any morphism with the universal property of the
blow-up along $`I` ({bpref "def:blowup_along"}[]), unique up to unique isomorphism over $`X`.
Smoothness over $`k` stands for
Hironaka's "non-singular", and the complement of the smooth locus for his singular locus; for a
scheme of finite type over a field of characteristic zero the two agree {cite Sta}[Tag 00TV]. His
"say reduced and irreducible" is the hypothesis that $`X` is integral. The smooth locus is open and
dense, so $`\pi` is birational.
::::

:::proof "thm:standard_algebraic"
Main Theorem I ({bpref "thm:mt1"}[]) gives an ideal sheaf $`D` on $`X` with support the singular
locus whose blow-up $`\pi \colon X' \to X` is regular; the library's blow-up has the universal
property ({bpref "def:blowup_along"}[]). The blow-up of a locally Noetherian scheme along an
ideal sheaf is proper and an isomorphism over the complement of the support of $`D`; over a perfect field
the singular locus is the complement of the smooth locus, and a regular scheme locally of finite
type is smooth {cite Sta}[Tag 00TV].
:::

# Local monomialization of a real-analytic function
%%%
tag := "sec-standard-analytic"
%%%

::::definition "def:monomial_at" (lean := "IsMonomialAt")
A real function $`g` on an $`n`-dimensional manifold $`M` is *a monomial at $`p` up to a unit*,
`IsMonomialAt n g p`, if near $`p`, in the coordinates $`y` of the chart of $`M` at $`p`,
$$`g = u \cdot y_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots y_n^{\alpha_n}`
for exponents $`\alpha_i \ge 0` and a real-analytic function $`u` that does not vanish at $`p`.
For $`n = 1` and the identity chart this is Mathlib's description of an analytic function of
order $`\alpha_1` at $`p` (`AnalyticAt.analyticOrderAt_eq_natCast`), with the coordinate not
centred at $`p`: a coordinate that does not vanish at $`p` contributes only a unit.
::::

::::theorem "thm:standard_analytic" (lean := "exists_proper_analytic_monomialization")
%%%
paperIdentity := some { label := "[Ati70, Resolution Theorem, p. 147]", href := "https://doi.org/10.1002/cpa.3160230202" }
%%%
Let $`f` be a real-analytic function on an open set $`U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n`, not identically
zero near any point of $`U`. Then there is a proper real-analytic map $`\pi \colon M \to U` from
an $`n`-dimensional real-analytic manifold $`M`, an analytic isomorphism over the set where $`f`
does not vanish, such that near every point $`p` of $`M`, in the coordinates $`y` of the chart of
$`M` at $`p`,
$$`f \circ \pi = u \cdot y_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots y_n^{\alpha_n}, \qquad \det D(\pi \circ y^{-1})(y) = v \cdot y_1^{\beta_1} \cdots y_n^{\beta_n}`
with $`u` and $`v` analytic and nonzero at $`p`.

This is {citeAs Ati70}[Atiyah's Resolution Theorem] over the whole of $`U` rather than near a point,
with Bierstone–Milman's monomial form of the Jacobian determinant in the same coordinates
{cite BM97}[Theorem 1.10]: the change of variables $`x = \pi(y)` turns integrals of functions of $`f`
into integrals of functions of monomials against monomial densities. Atiyah uses it to divide
distributions; Bernstein and Gelfand, independently, for the meromorphic continuation of
$`\int |P|^\lambda \varphi` {cite BG69}[]; and Watanabe for the asymptotics of Bayesian learning
{cite Wat09}[Theorem 2.3]. The manifold
$`M` is a type with Mathlib's charted-space structure modelled on $`\mathbb{R}^n` and analytic
chart changes, and the chart at $`p` is the one that structure prefers at $`p`.
::::

:::proof "thm:standard_analytic"
Bierstone–Milman's principalization ({bpref "thm:bm"}[]) applied to the principal ideal sheaf
$`(f)` on the manifold $`U`, which has nonzero stalks because $`f` is not identically zero near
any point, gives a proper analytic map $`\sigma \colon \tilde M \to U` which is an analytic
isomorphism off the zero set of $`f` and along which the pull-back of $`(f)`, generated by
$`f \circ \sigma`, is a normal-crossings divisor, and so is its product with the Jacobian ideal,
generated by the Jacobian determinant of $`\sigma`. At every point of $`\tilde M` the regular
system of parameters in which the product is a monomial consists of analytic functions with
independent differentials, which by the inverse function theorem are the coordinates of an analytic
chart; they are primes of the stalk, a unique factorization domain, so the two factors
$`f \circ \sigma` and the Jacobian determinant are units times monomials in the same coordinates.
The manifold $`M` is $`\tilde M` with these charts as its preferred charts.
:::
